<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Cell Biol</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Cell Biology</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0021-9525</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-8140</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4621586</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2108666</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>THE ORIENTATION OF DNA WITHIN 80-ANGSTROM CHROMATIN FIBERS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Dusenbery</surname><given-names>David B.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Uretz</surname><given-names>Robert B.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x32702e0N0x3b4efb0">From the Department of Biophysics, The University of Chicago, Chicago, Illinois 60637.</aff><author-notes><fn><p>Dr. Dusenbery's present address is the Division of Biology, California Institute of Technology, Pasadena, California 91109.</p></fn></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>3</month><year>1972</year></pub-date><volume>52</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>639</fpage><lpage>647</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>11</day><month>6</month><year>1970</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>8</day><month>11</month><year>1971</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1972 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Squashing salivary glands of <italic>Chironomus thummi</italic> larvae, <italic>Amblystoma tigrinum</italic> erythrocytes, or <italic>Spirostromum</italic> frequently results in stretched chromatin having highly oriented DNA as determined by polarized fluorescence microscopy of acridine orange-stained preparations. The examination of such material from <italic>C. thummi</italic> in the electron microscope indicates that the individual chromatin fibers have an average thickness of 80 A as is usually found in embedded and sectioned material. It is thus concluded that the DNA lies nearly parallel to the axis of these chromatin fibers. Detailed calculations of the polarization expected from various models of DNA packing are contained in an appendix. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>